{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WaGbeoCvkDZMkFuNDxGGXf","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — ec_hte_llm_annotation\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (via ARR)\n5\t- Deadline: ARR submission May 25, 2026; commitment deadline August 2, 2026\n6\t- Format: Long paper, 8 pages + unlimited references\n7\t\n8\t## Core Claims (3 条，可证伪)\n9\t\n10\t1. **Bias Mechanism Discovery**: When LLM misclassification rates for an effect modifier vary across subgroups, applying a global (pooled) correction to subgroup-level CATE estimates introduces systematic bias — over-correcting low-error subgroups and under-correcting high-error ones. In realistic settings, this \"debiasing\" can worsen CATE bias by 5-10x compared to no correction at all.\n11\t\n12\t2. **EC-HTE Solution**: Hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified correction (EC-HTE) eliminates this bias while controlling estimation variance through partial pooling. MVP demonstrates near-zero subgroup-level bias with simple stratification; HB partial pooling (exp-001) expected to further reduce RMSE by controlling matrix-inversion variance. Applicable to any CATE estimator; demonstrated with difference-in-means and CausalForestDML.\n13\t\n14\t3. **Diagnostic Criterion**: The product of misclassification heterogeneity × CATE heterogeneity across subgroups provides a computable diagnostic: when this product exceeds a threshold, global correction is harmful and stratified correction is needed. This criterion has standalone value independent of EC-HTE.\n15\t\n16\t## Story Spine\n17\t\n18\t- **Problem**: NLP researchers increasingly use LLM annotations as effect modifiers in heterogeneous treatment effect analysis (e.g., \"tweet contains economic framing\" moderates campaign effectiveness). LLM misclassification is non-random — error rates vary by subgroup (Ashwin et al., 2025: error varies by refugee status, gender, education). Current PPI/DSL corrections apply global rectifiers assuming homogeneous error.\n19\t\n20\t- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Mozer (2026) proves arm-specific rectifiers fix ATE bias under differential prediction error, but explicitly limits analysis to average treatment effects. PPI (Angelopoulos et al., 2023) and DSL (Egami et al., 2024) correct population-level estimates. PPCI (Sui et al., 2026) extends PPI to conditional functionals but corrects outcome-side measurement, not covariate-side. ForestIV (Bu et al., 2023) handles ML-covariate measurement error in regression, not CATE. **No work addresses correction when the mismeasured variable defines the heterogeneity structure.**\n21\t\n22\t- **Our framing**: We identify and formalize a previously unrecognized bias mechanism — measurement-error × causal-heterogeneity confounding. When a pooled confusion matrix is applied to correct subgroup-level CATEs, it misattributes prediction noise across subgroups with different error rates, producing bias proportional to the product of misclassification heterogeneity and CATE heterogeneity. This is a problem-level discovery, not a technique-level extension. EC-HTE solves it via hierarchical Bayesian stratified correction with honest causal forest integration. **Novelty positioning**: The individual components (HB Dirichlet estimation, confusion matrix correction, causal forests) are established; the contribution is (a) identifying and formalizing the bias mechanism specific to effect-modifier misclassification, (b) the Δπ×Δτ product characterization that predicts when correction backfires, and (c) bridging the measurement error and HTE literatures in a practically consequential way. Frame as \"problem identification + principled solution\" not \"new estimation technique.\"\n23\t\n24\t- **Headline result template**: \"On semi-synthetic political-science benchmarks, global correction worsens subgroup CATE bias by up to 7.8x in low-error subgroups; EC-HTE reduces bias to near-zero while maintaining 92-97% coverage, with as few as 250 expert labels.\"\n25\t\n26\t## Key Contributions (ordered for paper)\n27\t\n28\t1. **Bias mechanism identification + formal bound**: bias ∝ misclassification heterogeneity × CATE heterogeneity. Includes \"debiasing can hurt\" proposition with constructive example. (Theory + Empirical)\n29\t2. **EC-HTE framework**: Hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified misclassification correction + variance-adjusted honest causal forest. (Method)\n30\t3. **Diagnostic criterion**: Computable threshold from pilot data to decide whether stratified correction is needed. (Practical tool)\n31\t4. **Comprehensive validation**: Synthetic + semi-synthetic + real-data experiments with multiple LLMs. (Empirical)\n32\t\n33\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n34\t\n35\t1. **\"Why not just use expert labels directly for CATE?\"** — Defense: 2000 expert labels / k subgroups / honest forest halving → insufficient power per cell. EC-HTE uses all LLM data for low-variance CATE, expert labels only for calibration. Explicitly quantify in experiments.\n36\t\n37\t2. **\"How is this different from PPCI (Sui et al., 2026)?\"** — PPCI corrects outcome-side measurement error for conditional functionals via kernel localization. We correct covariate-side measurement error in the variable that defines the conditioning structure. Orthogonal problems.\n38\t\n39\t3. **\"Incremental over Mozer (2026)\"** — Mozer addresses ATE under differential prediction error. We address CATE under heterogeneous effect-modifier misclassification — a structurally different problem. The bias mechanism we identify (pooled correction hurting low-error subgroups) has no counterpart in Mozer's framework.\n40\t\n41\t4. **\"How sensitive to subgroup definition?\"** — Hierarchical Bayesian partial pooling makes the method robust: with well-defined subgroups, full stratification; with noisy subgroups, automatic shrinkage toward global. Include robustness analysis.\n42\t\n43\t5. **\"Real-world expert label cost\"** — Address with expert budget sensitivity sweep (n=100 to 2000) and adaptive allocation (Stage 3).\n44\t\n45\t6. **\"How does this differ from Carroll et al. (2006) / classical measurement error?\"** — Carroll et al. addresses measurement error in regression covariates; our contribution is the specific bias characterization for CATE estimation when the mismeasured variable *defines* the heterogeneity structure. The Δπ×Δτ product form is a new result in this context. Engage deeply with measurement error literature (Carroll, Gustafson, Hu & Schennach) and Bayesian annotation models (Dawid & Skene, Paun et al.) to contextualize precisely.\n46\t\n47\t7. **\"Methodological novelty is modest\"** — Acknowledge explicitly. Position contribution as problem discovery + formal characterization + comprehensive validation, not as a new estimation technique. The \"never worse than global\" guarantee via HB partial pooling is the key methodological property.\n48\t\n49\t8. **\"Why no figures?\"** — Paper MUST include key figures: (1) schematic of bias mechanism (global over/under-correction), (2) bias amplification heatmap (Δπ vs Δτ), (3) diagnostic ROC curve, (4) budget crossover curves. Move P2 experiments (exp-008, exp-009) to appendix to free space.\n50\t\n51\t## Venue Fit\n52\t\n53\tEMNLP fit is strong: core NLP application (LLM annotation quality), computational social science use case (political messaging, refugee interviews), direct comparison with NeurIPS/EMNLP published methods (DSL, PPI, benchmarking). Methodology is causal inference but framed through NLP lens. Potential reviewer pool: CL+CSS track.\n54\t\n55\tRisk: pure statistics reviewers may want stronger theoretical contribution (convergence rates, minimax bounds). Mitigation: emphasize the practical diagnostic criterion and empirical validation breadth.\n56\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"92734392-fd09-4d44-a705-e9afb826e672","uuid":"1fcfd540-c42a-499f-84d6-e7799a15a9dc","timestamp":"2026-05-20T20:02:20.775Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520/projects/ec_hte_llm_annotation/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T194535/best/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — ec_hte_llm_annotation\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (via ARR)\n- Deadline: ARR submission May 25, 2026; commitment deadline August 2, 2026\n- Format: Long paper, 8 pages + unlimited references\n\n## Core Claims (3 条，可证伪)\n\n1. **Bias Mechanism Discovery**: When LLM misclassification rates for an effect modifier vary across subgroups, applying a global (pooled) correction to subgroup-level CATE estimates introduces systematic bias — over-correcting low-error subgroups and under-correcting high-error ones. In realistic settings, this \"debiasing\" can worsen CATE bias by 5-10x compared to no correction at all.\n\n2. **EC-HTE Solution**: Hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified correction (EC-HTE) eliminates this bias while controlling estimation variance through partial pooling. MVP demonstrates near-zero subgroup-level bias with simple stratification; HB partial pooling (exp-001) expected to further reduce RMSE by controlling matrix-inversion variance. Applicable to any CATE estimator; demonstrated with difference-in-means and CausalForestDML.\n\n3. **Diagnostic Criterion**: The product of misclassification heterogeneity × CATE heterogeneity across subgroups provides a computable diagnostic: when this product exceeds a threshold, global correction is harmful and stratified correction is needed. This criterion has standalone value independent of EC-HTE.\n\n## Story Spine\n\n- **Problem**: NLP researchers increasingly use LLM annotations as effect modifiers in heterogeneous treatment effect analysis (e.g., \"tweet contains economic framing\" moderates campaign effectiveness). LLM misclassification is non-random — error rates vary by subgroup (Ashwin et al., 2025: error varies by refugee status, gender, education). Current PPI/DSL corrections apply global rectifiers assuming homogeneous error.\n\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Mozer (2026) proves arm-specific rectifiers fix ATE bias under differential prediction error, but explicitly limits analysis to average treatment effects. PPI (Angelopoulos et al., 2023) and DSL (Egami et al., 2024) correct population-level estimates. PPCI (Sui et al., 2026) extends PPI to conditional functionals but corrects outcome-side measurement, not covariate-side. ForestIV (Bu et al., 2023) handles ML-covariate measurement error in regression, not CATE. **No work addresses correction when the mismeasured variable defines the heterogeneity structure.**\n\n- **Our framing**: We identify and formalize a previously unrecognized bias mechanism — measurement-error × causal-heterogeneity confounding. When a pooled confusion matrix is applied to correct subgroup-level CATEs, it misattributes prediction noise across subgroups with different error rates, producing bias proportional to the product of misclassification heterogeneity and CATE heterogeneity. This is a problem-level discovery, not a technique-level extension. EC-HTE solves it via hierarchical Bayesian stratified correction with honest causal forest integration. **Novelty positioning**: The individual components (HB Dirichlet estimation, confusion matrix correction, causal forests) are established; the contribution is (a) identifying and formalizing the bias mechanism specific to effect-modifier misclassification, (b) the Δπ×Δτ product characterization that predicts when correction backfires, and (c) bridging the measurement error and HTE literatures in a practically consequential way. Frame as \"problem identification + principled solution\" not \"new estimation technique.\"\n\n- **Headline result template**: \"On semi-synthetic political-science benchmarks, global correction worsens subgroup CATE bias by up to 7.8x in low-error subgroups; EC-HTE reduces bias to near-zero while maintaining 92-97% coverage, with as few as 250 expert labels.\"\n\n## Key Contributions (ordered for paper)\n\n1. **Bias mechanism identification + formal bound**: bias ∝ misclassification heterogeneity × CATE heterogeneity. Includes \"debiasing can hurt\" proposition with constructive example. (Theory + Empirical)\n2. **EC-HTE framework**: Hierarchical Bayesian subgroup-stratified misclassification correction + variance-adjusted honest causal forest. (Method)\n3. **Diagnostic criterion**: Computable threshold from pilot data to decide whether stratified correction is needed. (Practical tool)\n4. **Comprehensive validation**: Synthetic + semi-synthetic + real-data experiments with multiple LLMs. (Empirical)\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n1. **\"Why not just use expert labels directly for CATE?\"** — Defense: 2000 expert labels / k subgroups / honest forest halving → insufficient power per cell. EC-HTE uses all LLM data for low-variance CATE, expert labels only for calibration. Explicitly quantify in experiments.\n\n2. **\"How is this different from PPCI (Sui et al., 2026)?\"** — PPCI corrects outcome-side measurement error for conditional functionals via kernel localization. We correct covariate-side measurement error in the variable that defines the conditioning structure. Orthogonal problems.\n\n3. **\"Incremental over Mozer (2026)\"** — Mozer addresses ATE under differential prediction error. We address CATE under heterogeneous effect-modifier misclassification — a structurally different problem. The bias mechanism we identify (pooled correction hurting low-error subgroups) has no counterpart in Mozer's framework.\n\n4. **\"How sensitive to subgroup definition?\"** — Hierarchical Bayesian partial pooling makes the method robust: with well-defined subgroups, full stratification; with noisy subgroups, automatic shrinkage toward global. Include robustness analysis.\n\n5. **\"Real-world expert label cost\"** — Address with expert budget sensitivity sweep (n=100 to 2000) and adaptive allocation (Stage 3).\n\n6. **\"How does this differ from Carroll et al. (2006) / classical measurement error?\"** — Carroll et al. addresses measurement error in regression covariates; our contribution is the specific bias characterization for CATE estimation when the mismeasured variable *defines* the heterogeneity structure. The Δπ×Δτ product form is a new result in this context. Engage deeply with measurement error literature (Carroll, Gustafson, Hu & Schennach) and Bayesian annotation models (Dawid & Skene, Paun et al.) to contextualize precisely.\n\n7. **\"Methodological novelty is modest\"** — Acknowledge explicitly. Position contribution as problem discovery + formal characterization + comprehensive validation, not as a new estimation technique. The \"never worse than global\" guarantee via HB partial pooling is the key methodological property.\n\n8. **\"Why no figures?\"** — Paper MUST include key figures: (1) schematic of bias mechanism (global over/under-correction), (2) bias amplification heatmap (Δπ vs Δτ), (3) diagnostic ROC curve, (4) budget crossover curves. Move P2 experiments (exp-008, exp-009) to appendix to free space.\n\n## Venue Fit\n\nEMNLP fit is strong: core NLP application (LLM annotation quality), computational social science use case (political messaging, refugee interviews), direct comparison with NeurIPS/EMNLP published methods (DSL, PPI, benchmarking). Methodology is causal inference but framed through NLP lens. Potential reviewer pool: CL+CSS track.\n\nRisk: pure statistics reviewers may want stronger theoretical contribution (convergence rates, minimax bounds). Mitigation: emphasize the practical diagnostic criterion and empirical validation breadth.\n","numLines":56,"startLine":1,"totalLines":56}}}
